     0.397  DBLP_conf_cav_SinghS12.pdf#p1#c7751

-- ANSWER --
Storyboard programming is a synthesis framework for helping programmers write “imperative low-level data-structure manipulations.” The paper says its goal is “to bridge the gap between the ‘boxes-and-arrows’ diagrams that programmers often use to think about data-structure manipulation algorithms and the low-level imperative code that implements them.”

It synthesizes code from a storyboard, which consists of:
- “a set of scenarios, each of which corresponds to an abstract input-output pair”
- “a set of fold and unfold definitions”
- “a skeleton of the looping structure of the desired algorithm”

The framework then “encod[es] both the synthesis and the abstract interpretation problem as a constraint satisfaction problem whose solution defines the desired low-level implementation.” In other words, it uses the storyboard information and solves constraints to generate the implementation.

The paper also says it can take “a set of partial input-output examples, as well as a description of the high-level structure of the desired solution,” and from that “synthesize low-level imperative implementations in a matter of minutes.”

